Our first observed agent application uses the [Markdown workshop page](/tokenmaxx) and [llms.txt](/llms.txt). The request reconstruction contains three agent requests: two reads, then one POST.
GET reads a page. POST sends application data.

| Relative to the first request | Request |
| --- | --- |
| Start | GET /tokenmaxx with text/markdown |
| 0.263 seconds later | GET /llms.txt |
| 18 minutes 16 seconds later | POST /api/joinInterest |

The reconstruction records:

- No requests for OpenAPI, AGENTS.md, lore, or MCP.
- One POST and no retry.
- A 200 submission response.
- Confirmation 88 seconds after submission.

## What the requests do not tell us

The 18-minute gap fits the [page's interview and approval flow](https://github.com/joelhooks/rat-stack/blob/main/apps/site/content/tokenmaxx.md). The reconstruction contains neither the agent's questions nor the person's answers.

Browser activity and agent requests are linked by timing only. The user agent does not name the software running the agent. We cannot identify which agent runs the calls.

This is one counts-only observation from our request analytics. Other calls may need [OpenAPI](/lore/openapi-describes-the-refusal-too) or [MCP](/lore/mcp-is-another-surface).

[Interest signup](/systems/interest) keeps the consent and email-confirmation rules. [HATEOAS](/lore/hateoas) explains why the page carries the next action.
